pub fn purpose -> String =
  "Use std/char to inspect a symbol’s Unicode number or construct a character from a received number. Char represents one scalar, a permitted Unicode code point. Single-quoted '👍' is Char; double-quoted \"👍\" is String. A visible character can contain several scalars, so first choose the unit the task needs."

pub fn reading -> String =
  "The import gives std/char the local name char, and let names the Char symbol. char.codePoint symbol returns the number 128077. A gives 65, and the null character written \\0 gives 0. Arguments follow a function name separated by spaces. pub effect fn main is the entry; println prints the result and a newline. Backticks form a string whose ${...} sections insert values and whose written line breaks appear in the output."

pub fn choose -> String =
  "codePoint converts Char to an Int number. fromCodePoint converts an Int to a result that may contain a Char. toString turns Char into a String containing the same one scalar, ready for a text label. None changes the original value."

pub fn checked -> String =
  "Not every Int can form a character. The result Maybe<Char> from fromCodePoint is either Just containing a Char or Nothing for an unusable number. The describe function uses match to choose a message for each alternative."

pub fn checkedReading -> String =
  "In fn describe, point: Int is the integer argument and the final -> String is the message’s result type. Just value names the contained Char value, then char.toString converts it to text. The Nothing branch prevents an invalid number from being silently dropped. 128077 becomes 👍, while the next four numbers are out of range or surrogates. The last two lines use True to confirm that zero and the maximum scalar number are accepted."

pub fn comparison -> String =
  "TypeScript also has String.fromCodePoint, but it accepts surrogates, the numbers used for UTF-16 encoding. Here fromScalar checks integer and range and excludes those surrogates, returning undefined on failure. describe handles undefined, corresponding to the Nothing branch."

pub fn comparisonBoundary -> String =
  "The same inputs exercise success and failure. The spelling true versus True is only an output-format difference. The last line shows that the JS built-in alone accepts surrogate 55296. JavaScript has no Char type, and Strings can be empty or contain more text, so do not assume every String contains one scalar."

pub fn limits -> String =
  "Scalars range from 0 through 0x10FFFF, excluding 0xD800 through 0xDFFF; 0x denotes hexadecimal. An accepted number need not have an assigned display glyph. These operations work on process and browser targets without an external service."

pub fn literals -> String =
  "'e\\u{0301}' contains e plus a combining accent, two scalars, so it is rejected as a Char literal with SES-P0202. Preserve that spelling by using the String \"e\\u{0301}\". The surrogate escape in '\\u{D800}' is rejected with SES-P0201. Choose different APIs when the task needs a visible piece rather than individual scalars."
